<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acts of the Apostles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Acts of the Apostles is the fifth book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, written by the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, probably late in the 1st century CE. It tells of the early Christian community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the missions of Peter and Paul across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a> had the apostle James executed and describes Agrippa's sudden death at Caesarea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles.jpg" alt="Acts of the Apostles" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin,_Staatliche_Museen_zu_Berlin_MS_Uncial_8683_verso_Acts_5,_12%E2%80%9321.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Acts is the second part of a two-volume work, Luke–Acts, whose first part is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>. Together the two volumes make up 27.5 percent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, the largest share attributed to a single author. The author is not named in the text. Church tradition from the 2nd century identifies him as Luke, a companion of Paul, and the first-person passages in Acts 16 to 27 have led many to read him as a participant in Paul's journeys. Critical opinion on the traditional attribution has been roughly evenly divided, and some scholars point to differences from Paul's own letters. The title was first used by Irenaeus in the late 2nd century and was not given by the author.</p><p>Most scholars date the work to 80 to 90 CE, because it uses the Gospel of Mark, looks back on the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and shows no awareness of Paul's letters. Others allow an early 2nd-century date, while some argue against a late date because the book says nothing of the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> or of Paul's trial before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Acts has 28 chapters and moves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It begins with the ascension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the replacement of Judas Iscariot by Matthias. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a> the Holy Spirit descends on the apostles, and the first believers share their property. After Stephen is stoned, persecution scatters the community, and the message reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> and the Gentiles. Saul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, a persecutor, is converted by a vision. Peter preaches to the centurion Cornelius, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> the followers of Jesus are first called Christians.</p><p>The second half follows Paul. The Gentile mission, promoted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, is confirmed at a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Paul travels through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, founding churches. After a mob attacks him in Jerusalem he is imprisoned, claims his right as a Roman citizen to be tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and is sent there by sea. He spends two years under house arrest, proclaiming the Kingdom of God. The book ends without recording the outcome of his legal troubles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Acts was read as a reliable history of the early church well into the post-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era. The debate over its historicity was most vehement between 1895 and 1915, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> argued that the author had rewritten history to present a united Peter and Paul. Most scholars now treat it as ancient historiography, with a theological purpose, and a middle range of scholars judge it relatively reliable by the standards of Hellenistic history writing. Its account agrees with Paul's letters on the outline of his career, though it differs on details.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acts of the Apostles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The movement soon spread to Jews of the Greek-speaking diaspora and to non-Jews, the Gentiles; in Antioch, according to the <b>Acts of the Apostles</b>, the believers were first called Christians.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Pupils learned the syllabary, then memorised the first chapter of the First Epistle of John in Ge'ez, then studied the <b>Acts of the Apostles</b>, and reaching the Psalms of David was celebrated by the parents with a feast.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 862/863, preparing for the mission, they developed the Glagolitic alphabet and translated the most important prayers and liturgical books, among them the Aprakos Evangeliar, the Psalter and the <b>Acts of the Apostles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Agrippa I courted Jewish opinion in Jerusalem, began a third city wall and, according to <b>Acts</b>, executed the apostle James; he died suddenly at Caesarea in 44, and Judea returned to Roman procurators.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a></li></ul></section>
